<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The husband challenged a trial court order directing him to pay the wife Rs 1,50,000 per month as maintenance. His case ran on three tracks: that his income from two commercial vehicles is only Rs 40,000 to 50,000 per month; that the trial court drew an adverse inference against him because his previous lawyer failed to file documents; and — the point that found prima facie traction — that the wife&rsquo;s own pleading had claimed Rs 1,00,000 per month, so the award of Rs 1,50,000 exceeded what was prayed for.</p>

<h2>The prima facie point: jurisdiction and the prayer</h2>
<p>The vacation Bench took the husband&rsquo;s affidavit of assets on record, subject to final adjudication, and recorded a prima facie finding that the trial judge exceeded jurisdiction by awarding more than the wife prayed for. On that footing the impugned order was stayed — but not unconditionally. The husband must pay an ad hoc Rs 1,00,000 per month until late December 2026 or further orders, with any excess or shortfall adjustable when the revision is finally decided. The matter now travels to the regular Bench.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Reading the order correctly:</strong> this is an interim stay on a prima facie view, not a declaration of law. The question whether a maintenance court may travel beyond the pleaded figure — given its duty to fix a fair and reasonable amount on the evidence — remains to be answered at final hearing.</p>
</div>

<h2>The competing principles</h2>
<p>Two strands pull against each other in cases like this. On one side, the general procedural discipline that a court does not ordinarily grant more than is claimed, and that a respondent shapes his defence to the case pleaded against him. On the other, the settled understanding that maintenance jurisdiction is a measure of social justice: the court assesses need and capacity on the affidavits and evidence, and pleadings in maintenance cases are often drafted early, before the true financial picture emerges through <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> disclosures. How a court balances the two — amendment of the claim, a reasoned finding on capacity, or restraint at the pleaded ceiling — is precisely the kind of question that gets settled in revisions such as this one.</p>

<h2>What the order means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For claimant wives.</strong> Plead the figure deliberately, and update it. If the disclosures show greater capacity than was known at filing, seek amendment or enhancement rather than relying on the court to travel beyond the prayer on its own.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For paying husbands.</strong> An award above the pleaded claim is a specific, arguable ground of challenge — but expect interim protection to come with payment conditions. Courts rarely stay maintenance outright; the ad hoc figure here matched the pleaded claim.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For both sides.</strong> The affidavit of assets remains the battlefield. Documents not filed at the right stage invite adverse inferences that are difficult to dislodge in revision.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Interim orders of this kind rarely make headlines, but they shape the real economics of matrimonial litigation: what is actually paid, month by month, while a revision pends. The order also illustrates sound revisional practice — identifying a single, crisp jurisdictional point for the stay, securing the wife through a substantial ad hoc payment, and keeping every contention open. Practitioners in Delhi will recognise the pattern from maintenance revisions before the High Court of Delhi, where stay applications are routinely decided on the same architecture of prima facie error plus protective conditions.</p>
